<div class="page-head">
  <h1>History &amp; Credits</h1>
</div>

<div class="prose">

<h2>Where this started</h2>
<p>ofxAddons began in 2011 as <a href="https://github.com/atduskgreg/ofxaddons.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">a project by Greg Borenstein</a> (<a href="https://github.com/atduskgreg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@atduskgreg</a>) &mdash; the original creator, and the person who had the idea in the first place: crawl Github for <code>ofx</code>-prefixed repositories and turn that convention into a real, browsable directory instead of something you had to already know to go searching for.</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/jamezilla" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@jamezilla</a> has kept that original project running as its sole maintainer since 2014, all the way through to today &mdash; over a decade of quietly keeping the lights on for a piece of infrastructure a huge amount of the openFrameworks community has relied on without necessarily knowing who to thank for it. Big ups to both of them.</p>
<figure class="prose__figure">
  <img src="/app/assets/img/ofxaddons-original-screenshot.jpg" alt="Screenshot of the original ofxAddons.com site, showing its Algorithms category listing" loading="lazy">
  <figcaption>The original <a href="https://www.ofxaddons.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ofxAddons.com</a>, still running today.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>This site</h2>
<p>This ofxAddons is a separate, independent build &mdash; not a fork of the original codebase &mdash; but it exists because that original project proved the idea was worth having: point a crawler at Github's <code>ofx</code> naming convention, and the openFrameworks addon ecosystem organizes itself. Everything here, from the category browser down to the naming convention it relies on, is a direct continuation of that idea.</p>
<p>See <a href="/about-openframeworks">About openFrameworks</a> for what the toolkit itself is, or <a href="/pages/howto">How To</a> for how addons and this directory work day to day.</p>

</div>
